            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Bread primarily symbolizes Jesus Christ (the Bread of Life) and his body given in the Eucharist, while also serving as a multivalent symbol for the Word of God, daily physical provision, and communal fellowship. Unleavened bread typically represents purity and sincerity, whereas leaven signifies corruption, sin, or false teaching.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Bread symbolizes the Word of God, with unleavened bread specifically representing pure doctrine and leavened bread representing corrupting doctrine.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Jesus Christ/Eucharist (primary), the Word of God, physical sustenance, and purity/corruption.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Word of God, and pure versus corrupt doctrine.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol of bread entirely to the Word of God and doctrine, excluding the primary consensus meanings of Christ's body, the Eucharist, physical provision, and communal fellowship.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction of the referents chosen, as 'Word of God' and 'corrupt doctrine' (leaven) are well-attested in the consensus. The disagreement lies in the book's exclusion of the dominant christological and sacramental meanings.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By selecting established alternative meanings from the consensus (bread as the Word of God, leaven as false doctrine) and making them the exclusive definitions, the book narrows the scope of the symbol, dropping its most prominent historical and biblical associations with Jesus Christ.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Gospel accounts consistently and explicitly identify bread with Jesus himself ('I am the bread of life', 'This is my body'). Omitting the christological and eucharistic referents ignores the most central New Testament usage of the symbol.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
